WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.679
An Ollama installation should be treated as a managed service,

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:05.826
not just a one-time tool download.

00:00:05.826 --> 00:00:10.837
Teams should know how it was installed, how the service starts, how it stops,

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:15.132
which user account it runs under, and where its runtime state lives.

00:00:15.132 --> 00:00:19.068
Those details matter when troubleshooting a failed startup, a missing model,

00:00:19.068 --> 00:00:22.289
a permission problem, or a configuration mismatch between machines.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:27.658
Service lifecycle practices are especially important when a local tool becomes part of shared work.

00:00:27.658 --> 00:00:29.805
A developer workstation, a lab server,

00:00:29.805 --> 00:00:33.026
and a production-like host may all need different settings.

00:00:33.026 --> 00:00:34.816
A workstation may favor flexibility.

00:00:34.816 --> 00:00:39.111
A shared server may need predictable startup, controlled network exposure, documented storage,

00:00:39.111 --> 00:00:40.184
and support expectations.

00:00:40.184 --> 00:00:43.763
A production-like host may require tighter change control and monitoring.

00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:48.774
Environment variables and service configuration should be documented because small differences can change behavior.

00:00:48.774 --> 00:00:51.995
Storage locations, bind addresses, model cache behavior, runtime directories,

00:00:51.995 --> 00:00:54.500
and service users can all affect operations.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.005
If one machine behaves differently from another,

00:00:57.005 --> 00:01:01.300
the answer is often in configuration rather than in the model itself.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.595
Logs and service status checks are the first place to look when

00:01:05.595 --> 00:01:08.100
the runtime does not behave as expected.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.679
Clear lifecycle practices make upgrades, restarts, troubleshooting, and support easier.

00:01:11.679 --> 00:01:16.689
They also help new team members understand the environment without depending on tribal knowledge.

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:20.626
It is also helpful to separate development assumptions from shared-service assumptions.

00:01:20.626 --> 00:01:25.279
A developer may restart a local service freely, change settings for a test,

00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:27.426
or remove models to save space.

00:01:27.426 --> 00:01:32.079
A shared host needs a more predictable process so active users, dependent applications,

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:35.300
and support staff understand what is changing and when.
